syntax:
typedef original_type alias_name;  // Example: typedef std::set<int> IntSet;

Alternatively, using the C++11+ syntax: using alias_name = original_type;  // Example: using IntSet = std::set<int>;

Implementation notes: The compiler processes typedef during the declaration phase, storing the alias in the symbol table for subsequent lookups. This ensures that when alias_name is used, it's substituted inline without generating any metadata or runtime checks, preserving the original type's memory layout (e.g., for std::set<int>, this includes the node-based structure with pointers for the balanced tree).

code_example:
#include <set>
#include <iostream>

typedef std::set<int> IntSet;  // Defines a type alias for std::set<int>

int main() {
    IntSet mySet;  // Compiler substitutes IntSet with std::set<int>
    mySet.insert(42);  // Invokes std::set<int>::insert
    for (const auto& elem : mySet) {
        std::cout << elem << std::endl;  // Iterates over the set
    }
    return 0;
}
